      "content": "I see. You're making a strong case for the pathway approach, but you're basically dismissing the enforcement side as something that just \"negates\" economic benefits. \n\nA lot of lawmakers would argue that without a secure border first, any humanitarian pathway is just an invitation for more chaos and a complete breakdown of the rule of law, which is itself a massive social cost. If the \"foundation\" isn't security, doesn't the whole system risk collapsing? How can you say the pathway side is the better foundation when the lack of enforcement itself creates the instability you're trying to solve?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
